   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Any luck yet? I have the same question, especially how to get the ECV in lavaan or is this the same as in the output under &#39;<span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Times;font-size:medium;text-align:-webkit-center;text-decoration-line:underline\">Latent Variances&#39; ??</span><div><div style=\"text-align:left\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times\" size=\"3\"><u>Please let me know.</u></font></div><div style=\"text-align:left\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times\" size=\"3\"><u><br></u></font></div><div style=\"text-align:left\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times\" size=\"3\"><u>G.</u></font></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div style=\"text-align:left\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times\" size=\"3\"><u><br></u></font></div><br>On Monday, 12 February 2018 15:57:37 UTC+1, Terrence Jorgensen  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"text-align:justify\"><b><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">1)<span style=\"font-weight:normal;font-stretch:normal;font-size:7pt;line-height:normal;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">     1) </span></span></b><b><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-repeat:initial\">Can the subscales be covariated with each other?</span></b></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>They can, as long as your model is empirically identified.  </div><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><b><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2)<span style=\"font-weight:normal;font-stretch:normal;font-size:7pt;line-height:normal;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">  2) </span></span></b><b><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-repeat:initial\">It seems that to validate the bifactor model, it is necessary to calculated the omega, Explained Common Variance (ECV) and Percent of Uncontaminated Correlations (PUC). </span></b><font face=\"Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>How can </b></span></font><b style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif;font-size:16px\">calculated </b><b style=\"font-size:16px;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">these indices in R? </b></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Versions of omega are available in the semTools package, using the<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> reliability() </font>function.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">UvA web page: <a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423028474000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0mbdgljnTeUCUH8GaFVpcZ\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div><div><br></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "eZjfEV4IAQAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2019-09-06T13:24:28Z",
   "subject": "Re: bifactor model",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">how to get the ECV in lavaan or is this the same as in the output under &#39;<span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Times;font-size:medium;text-align:-webkit-center\">Latent Variances&#39; ??</span></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>No, those would be variances of common factors.  Proportions of each observed-variable&#39;s variance explained by all predictors (i.e., all factors onto which it loads) can be found with<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> summary(fit, rsquare = TRUE)</font>.  But the ancient idea of a single combined ECV per &quot;factor&quot; comes from a principal-components and later EFA history, where they are all orthogonal.  That is not a general condition for CFAs, so although you bifactor model might very well have orthogonal factors.  If your factors are orthogonal, you could manually calculate the proportion of each observed-variable&#39;s variance uniquely explained by one factor.  The variance due to a factor (call it X) is that factor&#39;s variance times that item&#39;s squared loading.  The proportion is then X divided by (X + the item&#39;s residual variance).</div><div><br></div><div>You could ask on <a href=\"http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423028475000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2sIhDpCmHdiDGs5NOn9UiR\">SEMNET</a> or <a href=\"https://stats.stackexchange.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://stats.stackexchange.com/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423028475000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2fBYfbhv1ED9eJ-CrcCqVh\">CrossValidated</a> whether anyone knows how to find those indices, or how to calculate them manually from a model&#39;s estimates.</div><div><br></div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423028475000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2iMRtUG7oILRMM1Ncft6jZ\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
