     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thank you soo much for you answer. I&#39;ll look into it.<div><br></div><div>G. Williams</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Friday, 6 September 2019 15:24:28 UTC+2, Terrence Jorgensen  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">how to get the ECV in lavaan or is this the same as in the output under &#39;<span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Times;font-size:medium;text-align:-webkit-center\">Latent Variances&#39; ??</span></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div><div><div>No, those would be variances of common factors.  Proportions of each observed-variable&#39;s variance explained by all predictors (i.e., all factors onto which it loads) can be found with<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> summary(fit, rsquare = TRUE)</font>.  But the ancient idea of a single combined ECV per &quot;factor&quot; comes from a principal-components and later EFA history, where they are all orthogonal.  That is not a general condition for CFAs, so although you bifactor model might very well have orthogonal factors.  If your factors are orthogonal, you could manually calculate the proportion of each observed-variable&#39;s variance uniquely explained by one factor.  The variance due to a factor (call it X) is that factor&#39;s variance times that item&#39;s squared loading.  The proportion is then X divided by (X + the item&#39;s residual variance).</div><div><br></div><div>You could ask on <a href=\"http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423028475000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2sIhDpCmHdiDGs5NOn9UiR\">SEMNET</a> or <a href=\"https://stats.stackexchange.com/\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://stats.stackexchange.com/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423028475000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2fBYfbhv1ED9eJ-CrcCqVh\">CrossValidated</a> whether anyone knows how to find those indices, or how to calculate them manually from a model&#39;s estimates.</div><div><br></div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423028475000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2iMRtUG7oILRMM1Ncft6jZ\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
